# ===========================================================================
# HookuBit - CONTROL PLANE environment (NestJS, read by this service ONLY)
#
# WHAT THIS FILE IS
#   A template. It is copied to .env ONCE, on the first deploy, and never
#   again. After that the live file is the only thing that matters:
#
#       development   <repo>/apps/control-api/.env
#       production    /opt/hookubit/shared/apps/control-api/.env
#                     (symlinked into every release as
#                      <release>/apps/control-api/.env)
#
#   Editing THIS file changes nothing on a running box. Edit the live one.
#
# WHAT IS NOT IN IT
#   The variables the Go data plane also reads - APP_ENV, DATABASE_URL,
#   ENCRYPTION_KEY, ENCRYPTION_KEY_ID, ENCRYPTION_KEYS_RETIRED, REDIS_URL,
#   LOG_LEVEL, OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT, OTEL_SERVICE_NAMESPACE. Those live
#   in the repository-root .env, exactly once, so the two planes cannot drift
#   apart. Do not copy them here.
#
#   Anything that already has a default in code. A value restated in a template
#   becomes a silent fill-in: whatever is present here and absent from the
#   operator's file is what they get. Defaults are named in comments only.
#   Required variables appear with EMPTY values so validation refuses by name.
#
# HOW IT IS LOADED
#   envFilePath in src/app.module.ts is ['.env', '../../.env'] against a CWD of
#   apps/control-api, and EARLIER entries win - so anything set here overrides
#   the common file. systemd resolves it the same way round, listing the common
#   file first and this one second (later EnvironmentFile= wins).
#
# FORMAT RULES - BOTH PARSERS MUST AGREE, LINE FOR LINE
#   Read by dotenv (Node) AND systemd's EnvironmentFile=. Plain KEY=value only:
#   no quotes, no $, no export, no backslash continuations, no trailing
#   comments, no leading `;` on comments. The full reasoning is in the
#   repository-root .env.example; it applies identically here.
# ===========================================================================


# --- Secrets (REQUIRED, no default) ----------------------------------------
# Generate per environment and never commit real values:
#   openssl rand -base64 48
#
# Both must be at least 32 characters. JWT_SECRET signs access tokens;
# SESSION_SECRET signs the session cookie. Rotating either invalidates every
# live session, which is the intended behaviour after a leak.
JWT_SECRET=
SESSION_SECRET=

# --- Reverse proxies in front of this process (REQUIRED in staging/production)
# The EXACT number of reverse proxies between the internet and this process,
# used verbatim as Express's `trust proxy` hop count. Not a guess, not `true`.
#
#   2  Cloudflare -> nginx -> control-api   (the documented deployment)
#   1  nginx alone
#   0  nothing in front of this process at all
#
# Too low and X-Forwarded-For is ignored, so every client resolves to the proxy
# and ThrottleGuard's per-IP buckets collapse into ONE bucket for the whole
# internet - the per-IP limits on login and password reset stop existing, and a
# trickle of anonymous traffic locks every real user out. Too high (and `true`
# is unbounded) and a client prepends its own X-Forwarded-For entry to get a
# fresh bucket per request, which is worse than no rate limiting because it
# looks like there is some.
#
# There is deliberately no default. 0 is the safe value when the answer is
# unknown, but it is not a safe DEFAULT - a box behind nginx that simply never
# set the line got the collapsed-bucket failure silently. Under
# APP_ENV=staging or production the control plane REFUSES TO BOOT unless this
# is set, and 0 counts as set: say it out loud if it is true.
TRUST_PROXY_HOPS=

# --- Dashboard origin -------------------------------------------------------
# The base of every link the service mails - /verify-email?token=,
# /reset-password?token=, /accept-invitation?token=. A wrong value is a mailbox
# full of dead links.
#
# The scheme is REQUIRED and validated: app.example.com parses as a URL whose
# scheme is the hostname and would boot cleanly.
#
# There is deliberately no default. Unset, this used to fall back to
# http://localhost:5173, which on a server meant real password-reset and
# invitation mail carrying links to the operator's laptop - delivered, opened,
# and dead, with nothing refusing and nothing logged. Under APP_ENV=staging or
# production the control plane now REFUSES TO BOOT unless this is set, and
# http://localhost:5173 counts as set: say it out loud if it is true. Under
# development and test it is optional and reads as http://localhost:5173.
DASHBOARD_URL=

# --- Outbound mail ----------------------------------------------------------
# SET IS THE SWITCH. With SMTP_URL the SMTP transport is used in EVERY
# environment; without it, development and test fall back to a logging stub
# that delivers nothing, and staging and production REFUSE TO BOOT - a control
# plane that comes up healthy with signup, password reset and invitations
# silently dead is the failure that refusal exists to prevent.
#
# smtp://user:pass@host:587 or smtps://... The scheme is validated:
# host:587 is refused, because it parses as a URL whose scheme is the hostname.
# nodemailer query options work (?pool=true, ?ignoreTLS=true).
SMTP_URL=

# Required whenever SMTP_URL is set. `Display Name <address>` or a bare
# address; the display name is also the product name in subjects and bodies.
# Do NOT quote it - angle brackets and spaces are ordinary characters to both
# dotenv and systemd, and a quote would be parsed differently by each.
MAIL_FROM=

# --- Prisma migrations ------------------------------------------------------
# Migrations must bypass PgBouncer: transaction pooling breaks DDL and prepared
# statements. Set this to a DIRECT connection when the common file's
# DATABASE_URL points at a pooler. Empty is read as "not configured", so this
# line is safe to copy as-is.
#
# The Prisma CLI reads apps/control-api/.env and does NOT read the common file,
# so a migration run needs DATABASE_URL in the environment too - see
# docs/LOCAL_SETUP.md.
DIRECT_DATABASE_URL=

# --- Everything below has a safe default in code ----------------------------
# These are listed so they can be found, NOT set. An active line here is a
# value that ships itself to production; the default is in the comment.

# HTTP port this service listens on. Default: 3000.
# CONTROL_API_PORT=3000

# Comma-separated browser origins allowed to call the API cross-origin.
# Default: empty, which allows none - correct when the dashboard is served from
# the same origin or through a proxy.
# CORS_ORIGINS=

# Self-serve signup. Default: false, and it should stay false for any shared or
# internet-facing deployment. With it on, anyone who can reach
# /v1/auth/register creates an account and an organization they own - on a
# multi-tenant control plane that is an open door, not a feature. The first
# owner comes from `pnpm --filter @hookubit/control-api bootstrap` either way;
# there is NO default account (docs/adr/0006-no-default-credentials.md).
# ALLOW_OPEN_REGISTRATION=false

# Endpoint auto-disable. An endpoint whose circuit breaker has been
# CONTINUOUSLY open past the window is switched off, so a permanently dead
# endpoint stops accruing a delivery row for every matching event for ever, and
# an audit entry records why. Re-enabling is the ordinary POST .../enable,
# which arms a single breaker probe rather than releasing the backlog.
#
# The window is floored at 24h in code and is not adjustable downwards: a
# delivery's own wall-clock budget is max_retry_duration, 24h by default, so a
# shorter window would disable an endpoint while the deliveries it is failing
# are still legitimately being retried - and cancel them.
#
# Defaults: enabled true, after 72 hours, swept every 15 minutes, at most 200
# endpoints per pass. Setting ENABLED=false restores the old unbounded
# behaviour and says so loudly at boot.
# ENDPOINT_AUTO_DISABLE_ENABLED=true
# ENDPOINT_AUTO_DISABLE_AFTER_HOURS=72
# ENDPOINT_AUTO_DISABLE_INTERVAL_MINUTES=15
# ENDPOINT_AUTO_DISABLE_MAX_PER_RUN=200

# How often complete hours are rolled into usage_records. Default: 15 minutes.
# Being late costs nothing - the sweep looks back two days and fills whatever is
# missing, because the work is keyed by hour and idempotent. There is no off
# switch: the alternative to aggregating is counting a month of the largest
# table in the system every time somebody opens the billing page.
# USAGE_AGGREGATION_INTERVAL_MINUTES=15

# This process's `service.name` on every span. Default: control-api.
# Overridable because one install may run two control planes - staging and
# production - against a single collector, and OTEL_SERVICE_NAMESPACE alone
# does not separate them in every backend.
# OTEL_SERVICE_NAME=control-api

# Head sampling ratio, 0..1, parent-based. Default: 1. The control plane is the
# configuration and operator surface, deliberately NOT the delivery hot path
# (that is the Go data plane), so its request rate is small enough that
# sampling buys nothing and losing the one slow request costs a lot. A REMOTE
# parent is capped at this ratio rather than obeyed: `traceparent` is an
# unauthenticated request header and a caller must not choose how much we
# record.
# OTEL_TRACES_SAMPLER_ARG=1

# ===========================================================================
# LOCAL DEVELOPMENT
#
# Nothing required above is set, so a verbatim copy refuses to boot - which is
# the point for a server. For a laptop, after `pnpm dev:infra`, paste this
# block uncommented at the end of your apps/control-api/.env:
#
#   JWT_SECRET=<openssl rand -base64 48>
#   SESSION_SECRET=<openssl rand -base64 48>
#   DASHBOARD_URL=http://localhost:5173
#   CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173
#   DIRECT_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://hookubit:hookubit@localhost:5432/hookubit?schema=public
#   SMTP_URL=smtp://localhost:1025
#   MAIL_FROM=HookuBit <no-reply@localhost>
#
# TRUST_PROXY_HOPS is NOT needed under APP_ENV=development or test - it is
# required only for staging and production, and the same is true of
# DASHBOARD_URL (locally it reads as the http://localhost:5173 above whether or
# not you set the line). Vite proxies /v1 to :3000, so
# CORS_ORIGINS is belt-and-braces locally.
#
# Mailpit from the dev compose file catches every message: SMTP on 1025, inbox
# at http://localhost:8025. Nothing leaves the machine.
#
# The end-to-end suite additionally wants ALLOW_OPEN_REGISTRATION=true and the
# SMTP_URL above; see apps/dashboard/playwright.config.ts, which lists its
# prerequisites and starts none of them on purpose.
# ===========================================================================
